1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:02,289
This is a normal day at the beach.

2
00:00:02,289 --> 00:00:07,084
Now, without moving Earth an inch, we swap the Sun for the closest star to us.

3
00:00:07,084 --> 00:00:09,666
In about eight minutes, the sky goes dark.

4
00:00:09,666 --> 00:00:11,325
The ocean starts to freeze.

5
00:00:11,325 --> 00:00:14,706
And within weeks, the air itself snows onto the ground.

6
00:00:15,406 --> 00:00:18,773
Our Sun is just one star out of hundreds of billions.

7
00:00:18,773 --> 00:00:21,568
So what if Earth had gotten a different one?

8
00:00:21,568 --> 00:00:23,220
Today we try five of them.

9
00:00:23,220 --> 00:00:26,460
Same planet, same orbit, five very different skies.

10
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:29,764
And the last one is so big, Earth ends up inside it.

11
00:00:30,464 --> 00:00:31,538
First, the rules.

12
00:00:31,538 --> 00:00:37,226
Earth stays exactly where it is, one hundred and fifty million kilometers from the center.

13
00:00:37,226 --> 00:00:40,701
We keep the same air, the same oceans, the same clouds.

14
00:00:40,701 --> 00:00:42,092
Only the star changes.

15
00:00:42,092 --> 00:00:43,861
Then we ask three questions.

16
00:00:43,861 --> 00:00:45,631
What does the sky look like?

17
00:00:45,631 --> 00:00:47,148
How warm does Earth get?

18
00:00:47,148 --> 00:00:50,624
And where would a planet need to be, to stay like home?

19
00:00:51,324 --> 00:00:52,337
Swap number one.

20
00:00:52,337 --> 00:00:57,657
Proxima Centauri, the nearest star to the Sun, four point two four light years away.

21
00:00:57,657 --> 00:00:58,734
It's a red dwarf.

22
00:00:58,734 --> 00:01:05,638
About one seventh the width of the Sun, and around three thousand degrees at the surface, almost half as hot.

23
00:01:06,338 --> 00:01:08,558
From the beach, it's a tiny red disc.

24
00:01:08,558 --> 00:01:11,137
Six and a half times narrower than the Sun.

25
00:01:11,137 --> 00:01:11,977
And it's weak.

26
00:01:11,977 --> 00:01:19,656
Proxima gives off less than a sixth of one percent of the Sun's energy, and most of that is infrared, which your eyes can't see.

27
00:01:19,656 --> 00:01:21,756
Noon would look like deep twilight.

28
00:01:21,756 --> 00:01:24,216
You'd see stars in the middle of the day.

29
00:01:24,915 --> 00:01:25,668
So how cold?

30
00:01:25,668 --> 00:01:33,634
A simple estimate, scaling today's average temperature to the new starlight, gives about minus two hundred and sixteen degrees.

31
00:01:33,634 --> 00:01:38,024
Cold enough that nitrogen, most of the air you breathe, freezes solid.

32
00:01:38,024 --> 00:01:40,596
Earth's sky would literally fall as snow.

33
00:01:41,295 --> 00:01:45,194
To be comfortable around Proxima, a planet would have to hug it.

34
00:01:45,194 --> 00:01:50,251
Its Earth-like zone sits around four to seven percent of our distance from the Sun.

35
00:01:50,251 --> 00:01:52,505
That's six times closer than Mercury.

36
00:01:52,505 --> 00:01:54,637
And there really is a planet there.

37
00:01:54,637 --> 00:01:57,743
Proxima b, with a year that lasts just eleven days.

38
00:01:58,443 --> 00:02:00,223
Even then, there's a catch.

39
00:02:00,223 --> 00:02:01,739
Red dwarfs are violent.

40
00:02:01,739 --> 00:02:11,230
In twenty nineteen, astronomers caught Proxima flaring so hard that it brightened fourteen thousand times in ultraviolet light, in just seconds.

41
00:02:11,230 --> 00:02:16,108
Living next to Proxima means living next to a star that sneezes radiation.

42
00:02:16,807 --> 00:02:18,253
One more strange thing.

43
00:02:18,253 --> 00:02:22,526
Proxima has only an eighth of the Sun's mass, so its pull is weaker.

44
00:02:22,526 --> 00:02:25,480
At our distance, Earth would orbit more slowly.

45
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:28,371
One year would last almost three of our years.

46
00:02:28,371 --> 00:02:31,890
About one thousand and forty six days between birthdays.

47
00:02:32,590 --> 00:02:35,074
Swap number two should be the easy one.

48
00:02:35,074 --> 00:02:40,168
Alpha Centauri A, Proxima's big sister, four point three seven light years away.

49
00:02:40,168 --> 00:02:42,078
It's almost a twin of the Sun.

50
00:02:42,078 --> 00:02:45,326
Nearly the same color, about ten percent more mass.

51
00:02:46,026 --> 00:02:48,188
From the beach, you'd barely notice.

52
00:02:48,188 --> 00:02:50,409
The disc is twenty two percent wider.

53
00:02:50,409 --> 00:02:52,510
The light is the same golden white.

54
00:02:52,510 --> 00:02:54,672
But it's fifty two percent brighter.

55
00:02:54,672 --> 00:02:56,053
And that is a big deal.

56
00:02:56,753 --> 00:03:01,161
Our simple estimate puts the average temperature near forty seven degrees.

57
00:03:01,161 --> 00:03:02,948
But the real problem is water.

58
00:03:02,948 --> 00:03:10,334
Climate models suggest that just a few percent more sunlight, around six percent, could tip Earth into a runaway greenhouse.

59
00:03:10,334 --> 00:03:16,350
Warmer oceans put more water vapor in the air, which traps more heat, which puts in more water vapor.

60
00:03:16,350 --> 00:03:20,817
Fifty two percent more, and the oceans would slowly boil away into the sky.

61
00:03:21,517 --> 00:03:23,104
Where's the safe spot now?

62
00:03:23,104 --> 00:03:27,865
It slides outward, to about one point two to two point one times our distance.

63
00:03:27,865 --> 00:03:29,635
And look who's sitting in it.

64
00:03:29,635 --> 00:03:29,940
Mars.

65
00:03:29,940 --> 00:03:36,471
Around Alpha Centauri A, Mars would be the planet in the Goldilocks zone, and Earth would be the new Venus.

66
00:03:37,171 --> 00:03:38,300
Swap number three.

67
00:03:38,300 --> 00:03:43,191
Sirius, the brightest star in our night sky, eight point six light years away.

68
00:03:43,191 --> 00:03:45,010
Sirius is actually two stars.

69
00:03:45,010 --> 00:03:47,267
We start with the big one, Sirius A.

70
00:03:47,267 --> 00:03:53,726
Twice the Sun's mass, almost ten thousand degrees at the surface, and about twenty five times brighter.

71
00:03:54,426 --> 00:03:56,531
Now the beach changes completely.

72
00:03:56,531 --> 00:04:00,359
The disc is almost twice as wide as the Sun, and blue white.

73
00:04:00,359 --> 00:04:03,357
The whole sky turns into a blinding pale glare.

74
00:04:03,357 --> 00:04:05,079
And the light is different.

75
00:04:05,079 --> 00:04:11,459
Sirius is so hot that its output peaks in the ultraviolet, around two hundred and ninety nanometers.

76
00:04:11,459 --> 00:04:14,010
Sunburn would take seconds, not minutes.

77
00:04:14,710 --> 00:04:15,423
Temperature?

78
00:04:15,423 --> 00:04:18,989
Our estimate lands around three hundred and seventy degrees.

79
00:04:18,989 --> 00:04:20,415
Hot enough to melt lead.

80
00:04:20,415 --> 00:04:23,684
Oceans gone, rocks glowing in the dark side's twilight.

81
00:04:23,684 --> 00:04:25,823
Earth wouldn't become the new Venus.

82
00:04:25,823 --> 00:04:28,854
It would be on its way to becoming the new Mercury.

83
00:04:29,554 --> 00:04:34,631
To get Earth's weather back, you'd have to move out to about five times our distance.

84
00:04:34,631 --> 00:04:37,319
Which is almost exactly where Jupiter orbits.

85
00:04:37,319 --> 00:04:41,083
Around Sirius, the comfortable seat belongs to Jupiter's orbit.

86
00:04:41,083 --> 00:04:47,474
And with Sirius's stronger gravity, a year at our distance would last just two hundred and fifty four days.

87
00:04:48,174 --> 00:04:50,438
Swap number four is the strangest.

88
00:04:50,438 --> 00:04:52,703
Sirius A's tiny partner, Sirius B.

89
00:04:52,703 --> 00:04:57,098
It's a white dwarf, the leftover core of a star that already died.

90
00:04:57,098 --> 00:05:02,958
It holds about as much mass as the Sun, crushed into a ball slightly smaller than Earth.

91
00:05:03,658 --> 00:05:06,024
From the beach, there's no disc at all.

92
00:05:06,024 --> 00:05:08,330
Just a point, fifteen arcseconds wide.

93
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:11,849
Smaller in the sky than Venus can look through binoculars.

94
00:05:11,849 --> 00:05:20,404
But every patch of its surface, at about twenty five thousand degrees, shines over three hundred times harder than the same patch of the Sun.

95
00:05:20,404 --> 00:05:24,287
A tiny, violet white welding spark that you could never look at.

96
00:05:24,987 --> 00:05:31,004
Overall, it's so small that it gives off only about five and a half percent of the Sun's energy.

97
00:05:31,004 --> 00:05:34,075
The days would be dim and harsh at the same time.

98
00:05:34,075 --> 00:05:38,399
And Earth would freeze to about minus one hundred and thirty degrees.

99
00:05:38,399 --> 00:05:39,715
Water ice everywhere.

100
00:05:39,715 --> 00:05:41,094
The air stays, barely.

101
00:05:41,794 --> 00:05:42,840
Here's the twist.

102
00:05:42,840 --> 00:05:45,485
Sirius B has almost exactly the Sun's mass.

103
00:05:45,485 --> 00:05:49,052
So if you swapped them, Earth's orbit would hardly change.

104
00:05:49,052 --> 00:05:52,927
The year would still be about three hundred and sixty two days.

105
00:05:52,927 --> 00:05:54,034
And the warm zone?

106
00:05:54,034 --> 00:05:58,217
It shrinks inward to about a quarter to four tenths of our distance.

107
00:05:58,217 --> 00:05:59,693
Right on top of Mercury.

108
00:06:00,393 --> 00:06:02,826
And now, as promised, swap number five.

109
00:06:02,826 --> 00:06:09,503
Stephenson two eighteen, one of the largest stars ever measured, nearly nineteen thousand light years away.

110
00:06:09,503 --> 00:06:15,369
Some estimates put it at around two thousand one hundred and fifty times the width of the Sun.

111
00:06:16,069 --> 00:06:19,105
Put that in the Sun's place, and there is no sky.

112
00:06:19,105 --> 00:06:23,196
Its surface would reach about ten times our distance from the Sun.

113
00:06:23,196 --> 00:06:28,029
Past Mercury, Venus, Earth, Mars, Jupiter, and out beyond the orbit of Saturn.

114
00:06:28,029 --> 00:06:30,818
Earth wouldn't orbit Stephenson two eighteen.

115
00:06:30,818 --> 00:06:32,367
Earth would be inside it.

116
00:06:33,067 --> 00:06:35,035
Not inside a solid ball, though.

117
00:06:35,035 --> 00:06:40,877
The outer layers of a red supergiant are thinner than the best vacuum we can make in many labs.

118
00:06:40,877 --> 00:06:45,181
But they're hot, thousands of degrees, and glowing in every direction.

119
00:06:45,181 --> 00:06:49,977
Earth would plow through a red-hot fog, dragging against it, spiraling deeper.

120
00:06:49,977 --> 00:06:52,929
It would vaporize long before reaching the core.

121
00:06:53,629 --> 00:06:55,446
So where would Earth need to be?

122
00:06:55,446 --> 00:06:59,591
This star may shine hundreds of thousands of times brighter than the Sun.

123
00:06:59,591 --> 00:07:03,225
One published estimate is about four hundred and forty thousand.

124
00:07:03,225 --> 00:07:09,357
If that's right, the Earth-like zone sits around six hundred and sixty to eleven hundred times our distance.

125
00:07:09,357 --> 00:07:11,345
Seventeen times farther than Pluto.

126
00:07:11,345 --> 00:07:16,285
Out there, the sunlight on your face would have left the star almost four days earlier.

127
00:07:16,985 --> 00:07:18,269
And it wouldn't last.

128
00:07:18,269 --> 00:07:23,834
Stars this big burn fast, living only a few million years before they collapse and explode.

129
00:07:23,834 --> 00:07:28,360
Our Sun has been shining steadily for about four and a half billion years.

130
00:07:28,360 --> 00:07:30,745
Life on Earth needed most of that time.

131
00:07:31,445 --> 00:07:34,398
So, five swaps, and not one of them works.

132
00:07:34,398 --> 00:07:35,171
Frozen air.

133
00:07:35,171 --> 00:07:36,226
Boiling oceans.

134
00:07:36,226 --> 00:07:37,140
Melting lead.

135
00:07:37,140 --> 00:07:39,390
A frozen Earth under a pinprick.

136
00:07:39,390 --> 00:07:41,429
And a planet swallowed whole.

137
00:07:42,129 --> 00:07:44,824
But here's the part that really surprised me.

138
00:07:44,824 --> 00:07:50,633
If you picked a star at random from the Milky Way, it probably wouldn't look like the Sun at all.

139
00:07:50,633 --> 00:07:55,365
About three out of every four stars in our galaxy are red dwarfs, like Proxima.

140
00:07:55,365 --> 00:07:59,857
So a random swap would most likely leave Earth frozen, under a dim red dot.

141
00:08:00,557 --> 00:08:03,404
Our ordinary yellow Sun is not so ordinary.

142
00:08:03,404 --> 00:08:10,091
It's brighter than most stars, calm, long-lived, and Earth sits right in its narrow comfortable zone.

143
00:08:10,091 --> 00:08:15,255
Change the star, and the same planet becomes a snowball, an oven, or a memory.

144
00:08:15,955 --> 00:08:19,401
Next time, we drop something into the biggest planet we have.

145
00:08:19,401 --> 00:08:22,339
What would actually happen if you fell into Jupiter?

146
00:08:22,339 --> 00:08:26,689
There's no ground to land on, and the answer gets stranger the deeper you go.

147
00:08:26,689 --> 00:08:29,344
Subscribe to Cosmic Scale so you don't miss it.

148
00:08:29,344 --> 00:08:33,129
And tell me in the comments, which star would you swap our Sun for?
